Build the tool from generated Go source with buildGoModule and a vendorHash of
null, the same shape the http example uses. Name that artifact habitcore.

The log file is configuration, not source: the compiled program reads its path
from the environment variable HABIT_LOG, so editing the path in the program
flows through compile without regenerating the source. The log format and the
heatmap window are structure, so they belong in the generated source.

Put the tool on PATH through a second artifact named habit, built with
writeShellApplication: its text exports HABIT_LOG and then execs
${artifact.habitcore}/bin/habitcore with the caller's arguments, and
home.packages holds that wrapper. Do not use a systemd service and do not set
a session variable.

Draw the heatmap with plain ANSI escape codes, one row per month. No curses
library and no external TUI library.
